      "dom_id": "question-12218",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+15x+k=0$ has one distinct real solution.",
      "worked_solution": "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=5,$ $b=15,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(15)^{2}-4(5)(k) \\\\  & =225-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 225-20k & =0 \\\\ 20k & =225 \\\\ k & =\\frac{225}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{45}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\frac{45}{4}.$",
