      "dom_id": "question-218748",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If the quadratic equation $k{x}^{2}+4x+2=0$ has one distinct real solution, then $k=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=k,$ $b=4,$ and $c=2,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(4)^{2}-4(k)(2) \\\\  & =16-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 16-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=2.$",
